A||N||4||Hank is Here||PF47 V PF52 AC filters||||1||
Z||000000||Hank is Here||11-13-2000||11:12 PM||||Okay, <BR> Here is what I know.  Th books say the PF47 is the correct filter for my 87 GT, and the correct filter for my fathers 88 GT is the P52.  These filters appear to be the same for mounting reasons and in the diameter as well.  The PF52 filter is about one inch lnger or so.  Can I safely use the PF52 on my 87 because it has a larger filter surface area.  Basically are the pressure bypass valves set the same?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||11-14-2000||05:46 AM||||I've been using the PF52 for the 3.8L in my 85 Regal for years and in my 2.5L Fiero too.  Both recommend the PF47.<P>I've had no problems.  The larger oil capacity, if anything, should be good.<P>If there is enough clearance for the PF52 in the V6's, I would think there shouldn't be any problems using it.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-14-2000||08:15 PM||||I don't see any reason to use the 47 unless the 52 won't clear something. The recall data for 84 4 cyl also called for a bigger filter to be used to increase oil capacity.<P>Actually if you dig around the parts books you'll find that castrol and others using Fram numbering often use xxx3980 which is the PF 52 equivalent. <P>Fram does have a 47 eqivalent filter, xxx3987, but not all other brands have it. For example you can't get the short filter if you want a castrol filter.<P>I use the long filter on my 4 cyl. It's allot easier to get changed but it does increase oil system capacity some.<P>As for the bypass. it's there for when the oil is really cold or the filter is really dirty. If the thing opens due to dirt you've got something pretty bad going on, especially on the 52.||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||11-14-2000||08:29 PM||||Even though I have a Fiero I am not familiar with the engines in them.I have never seen an engine without an oil pressure relief valve in it and I have never seen an oil filter with a relief.Some filters have what is basically a check valve in the form of a rubber seal which keeps the filter from draining when the engine is off so you don't start with an empty oil filter.If your filter should plug for some reason,the engine's relief valve will come into play and dump excess pressure into the crankcase.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-14-2000||11:32 PM||||Hugh, I think he means the bypass valve inside the filter. it opens if the filter is really cruddy or the oil is to cold to pass thru the filter media. This valve prevents oil starving and colapsed media.||||reg||1
